  "body": "While looking at https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/7658 I noticed that the `Link` HTTP header will process the `media` header attribute for `rel=preconnect` and `rel=preload`, but the [`<link rel=preconnect>`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/links.html#link-type-preconnect) steps don't say to do anything with the `media` attribute.\r\n\r\nHowever, https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#processing-the-media-attribute says \r\n\r\n> However, if the link is an [external resource link](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/links.html#external-resource-link), then the [media](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#attr-link-media) attribute is prescriptive. The user agent must apply the external resource when the [media](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/semantics.html#attr-link-media) attribute's value [matches the environment](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/common-microsyntaxes.html#matches-the-environment) and the other relevant conditions apply, and must not apply it otherwise.\r\n\r\nThis is a bit action at a distance, and \"must not apply\" doesn't clearly mean \"do not preconnect\" or \"do not fetch\". (In particular, for `stylesheet` the resource is fetched and parsed as CSS but then \"not applied\" as per CSS rules.)",
